  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/l102_hypancistrus_inspector_en/",
      "html": "<p>The Snowball Pleco (Hypancistrus inspector, L102) can reach a maximum length of about 18-20 cm and is one of the largest species in Hypancistrus.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/l102_hypancistrus_inspector_en/",
      "html": "<p>The species comes from the Rio Negro in Brazil.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/l102_hypancistrus_inspector_en/",
      "html": "<p>Most L102 have large white spots on the body and dorsal fin, hence the popular name snowball pleco. However, very finely spotted individuals, dashed ones and almost black ones also occur. L102 can always be easily recognised by the dark seam of the dorsal fin, which makes the species unmistakable. All these colour morphs therefore belong to H. inspector. This is another good example that plecos can be very variable in individual pattern, and that one should not rely too much on pattern for determining the species.</p>"
    }
  ],
